00:03:44.603 --> 00:03:45.450
Let's dive into this story.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:46.920
It's a big one, it's an important one,

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.677
and I'm excited to share it with you.

00:03:48.677 --> 00:03:50.580
(mysterious music)

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:51.930
The economy that we live in,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.870
that we all take for granted,
is actually relatively new.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:57.240
The clothes that you're
wearing right now, the computer

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.430
or the phone that you're
looking at to watch this video,

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.140
the materials that your
house is made out of,

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.420
the fibers in the blanket
that you use at night,

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.730
the food you eat, all of it is a product

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.130
of perhaps one of the most
significant achievements

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.779
of our species: global trade.

00:04:10.779 --> 00:04:13.320
(mysterious music)
(camera shutter clicking)

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.020
Early on, humans discovered
the magic that occurs

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:18.180
when a group of people specialize

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.880
in producing something
valuable like grain.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.410
They make more than they need,

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:23.670
and then they take that excess

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.200
and they give it to another group

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.450
who also made too much of something

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.810
because they specialize in a
specific thing, maybe textiles.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:32.010
And through this exchange,

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.860
both parties benefit way more
than they could on their own.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.310
It was this kind of cooperation
that leveled our species up

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.560
beyond any other animal on Earth.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.810
So we just kept doing it.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.090
In fact, the history of
our human civilization

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.430
is largely the history of people trading

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.020
on an ever-growing scale.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:51.570
Today, this is normal.
We call it the economy.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.370
By the time the 1900s rolled around,

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:54.960
we had a pretty connected world.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.240
There was global trade all over the place,

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.710
but it was still pretty clunky.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:01.410
Everyone had different trade
rules, different currencies.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.200
Countries were worried that
foreign products would threaten

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.200
their domestic producers, so
they put up trade barriers

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.900
and imposed big tariffs on
stuff coming into their country.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.060
Then, 70 years, ago came that war

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.280
that almost tore this world apart.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.680
(explosions thunder)
(engines rumble)

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.270
Out of the ashes of that war,

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:21.510
the US emerged as the most
powerful country ever.

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:24.810
It had troops and influence
in every corner of the globe.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.100
And with that new power,

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.620
the US and its allies wrote a new playbook

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:29.790
for the world economy.
(dollar sign chimes)

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.470
They would make it so that the US dollar

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:32.550
was the top currency.

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:34.770
Everything would kind of
be pegged to the US dollar.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.720
This helped stabilize the whole system.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:38.370
It helped it move more predictably.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.670
It helped countries send and
receive money across borders.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.530
The US also got countries together

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.720
with the goal of lowering
the taxes imposed

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.750
on products flowing in and
out of their country: tariffs.

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.160
And through all these efforts,

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.410
all these new rules, borders opened up.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.080
Investors could put their
money almost anywhere on Earth

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.520
to grow new businesses.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:58.830
Corporations could look to far away lands

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:01.320
for cheaper workers to
set up their factories.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.720
The world became one big market,

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.828
and it started to benefit
hundreds of millions of people.

00:06:06.828 --> 00:06:08.670
(bright music)

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:12.330
So much so that even
anti-capitalist China couldn't stay

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:16.680
out of this global economy that
was so unrecognizably open,

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.000
that was so much more open

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.650
than anything the world had ever seen.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:22.500
There was way too much money
to be made, so China jumped in.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.120
They said that they
would remain communists,

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:26.790
but they would open up a few
dozen of their coastal cities

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.733
to be open for business
in this new global market.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.270
Millions of China's rural poor flocked

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.170
to the coastal cities, these
gateways to the global market.

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:40.020
They showed up ready to
work, and to work for cheap.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.210
A world away, over in the United States,

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.580
corporations looked at
all of their factories

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.740
in places like Detroit and Buffalo,

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.750
and realized that it would
make a lot more sense

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:51.060
for them to move all of
these factories to China

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.310
where they could pay workers
a fraction of the wage.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.680
And, spoiler alert, all
these coastal cities in China

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.640
that opened up to the global
economy soon looked like this.

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.340
Hundreds of millions of
people in China lifted out

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.970
of poverty and thrust into this
new middle and upper class,

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.580
all thanks to the magic
of the global economy

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:10.170
based on the rules and policies

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:12.270
that the US had set up after World War II.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.800
It was so good for China and
a bunch of other countries,

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.740
but for the many Americans
whose jobs had just been given

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:22.740
to someone in like Shanghai,
this didn't feel like magic.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:23.820
It felt like betrayal,

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:26.476
like a recipe for poverty and ghost towns.

00:07:26.476 --> 00:07:27.600
(haunting music)

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:30.690
This China shock was one
of the first big tastes

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:33.870
of the economic pain that this
global economy could inflict

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:35.520
on huge numbers of people.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.860
But to everyone else,
it was totally worth it,

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:39.390
because thanks to this system,

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:41.880
people were suddenly seeing
prices at their stores

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.010
drop further than seemed possible.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:48.210
Washing machines, shoes,
tools, toasters, couches,

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.880
shirts, socks, toys,
mattresses, lawn chairs,

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.550
anything you can think of is now flowing

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:54.750
from Chinese factories

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:57.660
and available for dirt
cheap at your local Walmart,

00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:59.850
all while transforming the lives of people

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:02.940
in places like Vietnam,
India, Chile, and Thailand,

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.580
who saw huge economic
growth during these years.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:08.340
So by the '80s, China's
fully in the global economy,

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:09.810
and it is a gravy train.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:11.700
The US and UK both elect leaders

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:15.060
who love this free-trade
global capitalism.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.940
- More trade, not less is
the wave of the future.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:19.140
- The new system was boosted

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:21.540
by all of this new technology
that was coming out,

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:24.180
making it easier to pass
information across borders,

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:25.830
to move money, to move people,

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:27.570
to move goods across the globe.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:28.403
A globe.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:06.150
Promise to obey.

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:08.640
If we all believe and obey these rules,

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:10.200
it creates the conditions

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:12.540
that made the world into
a giant marketplace,

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.880
where a corporation could
source whatever they needed

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.220
to make their product from
anywhere in the world,

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:19.110
whatever made the most economic sense.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:21.420
Soon, products as simple as a T-shirt

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:25.110
would travel along an
unfathomably complex supply chain,

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:26.970
using material and labor

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:28.950
from many countries all around the globe,

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:31.050
before eventually showing up conveniently

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:33.420
just in time at the store.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:36.390
Policy had created mass
cooperation between billions

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:38.370
of people who don't
speak the same language,

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:39.570
who don't know each other,

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:41.970
but are somehow all getting
in on the same market.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:44.760
This mental construct of economic policy

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:46.500
is what created globalization,

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.030
the globalization we know today.

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:49.470
And what's crazy to me is that,

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:52.410
if you tinker with the
policy, if you mess with this,

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:55.380
you can reverse a lot
of this globalization,

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.630
which is exactly what's
happening right now.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.570
- There is simply no reason why
the blades for wind turbines

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:03.000
can't be built in Pittsburgh
instead of Beijing.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.620
- Okay, but let's stick
to our timeline here.

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:05.910
We're kind of in the '90s.

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:08.640
And I'll tell you, people in
the '90s would've never guessed

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.530
what direction this story is about to go.

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:12.810
The Soviet Union had just fallen,

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:15.030
free trade and global capitalism

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:17.370
had unambiguously beat communism,

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:20.010
and the magic of the free
market was seen as the solution

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:21.270
for pretty much everything.

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:23.730
Free trade had just ended global conflict

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.280
between these great
superpowers, maybe forever.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:28.590
Free trade was eradicating global poverty.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:31.680
Free trade was pushing
countries to adopt democracy.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:32.910
And here's Bill Clinton,

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:34.590
giddy about how good it's all going,

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:37.500
predicting that free trade
would bring democracy to China.

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:40.080
- The more China liberalizes its economy,

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:43.590
the more fully it will liberate
the potential of its people,

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:46.020
their initiative, their imagination,

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:48.210
their remarkable spirit of enterprise.

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:50.700
And when individuals have
the power not just to dream,

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:51.990
but to realize their dreams,

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:54.240
they will demand a greater say.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:58.620
- Free trade would prevent
our age-old human curse, war,

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:00.720
by so deeply entangling countries

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:02.850
in mutually beneficial deals

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:04.320
that it wouldn't make sense to fight.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:06.510
After all, free trade had turned Europe

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:09.900
from a continent ravaged by
war to this tightly-knit group

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:12.540
where everyone's holding
hands and smiling together.

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:15.000
Why wouldn't this naturally
just happen to the whole globe?

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:16.890
China continued to make stuff

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:18.660
and send it to the United States.

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:21.960
Our dependency on them and
them on us continue to grow,

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:24.810
and that would surely
prevent any conflict, right?

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:27.540
Um, no.
(deflated music)

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:30.030
It's looking like that's
not how it's gonna turn out.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:32.640
In fact, it's kind of the opposite.

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:34.800
- The new tariffs from both sides

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:36.390
went into effect earlier this morning.

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:38.670
The US has imposed 10% tariffs

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:41.550
on $200 billion worth of Chinese imports.

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:44.833
- China is our military's
most consequential strategic

00:11:44.833 --> 00:11:46.980
competitor and pacing challenge.

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:48.960
- In a relatively sudden shift,

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.540
governments around the
world are undoing a lot

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:55.140
of this globalization, this
solution to all of our problems.

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:56.070
They're rejecting it.

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:58.680
India is paying companies
to keep their production

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.000
within their own borders,

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.350
especially for important industries

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:03.300
like pharmaceuticals and electronics.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:05.880
South Korea is giving
taxpayer money to companies

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:07.920
to produce green energy infrastructure

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:10.170
instead of waiting for the
free market to come build it.

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:13.860
Japan is paying companies to
move production back to Japan

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:16.140
and to move their supply
chains away from China.

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:17.850
Australia is using taxpayer money

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:20.940
to encourage mining companies
to establish the processing

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:24.390
of rare-earth mineral
facilities within their borders

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:25.470
instead of abroad.

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:28.590
Nigeria is imposing
restrictions on foreign goods

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:30.600
that they want to see
produced domestically,

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:32.250
like rice and cement.

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:34.290
They don't wanna be
reliant on other countries.

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:35.340
And then you've got Europe,

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:38.040
where tons of countries are
investing government money

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:40.020
and protecting homegrown industries

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:42.390
that have always been directed
by the global economy,

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:45.060
like energy, agriculture, and cars.

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:47.100
The government getting
involved in these markets

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:49.890
incentivizes companies
and consumers to make

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:51.990
and sell these products in country

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:54.810
as opposed to looking
abroad at the global market.

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:56.940
And of course, leading out on all of this

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:00.660
is the OG of the modern
free-market global economy,

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:03.330
the United States, who is
suddenly changing course

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:04.770
from how they've been operating,

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:06.570
all the way up until like Obama.

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:08.610
Like, Obama spent a great deal of his time

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:11.220
trying to increase free
trade, especially with Asia.

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:13.860
Then, first under Trump
and now under Biden,

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:16.560
the US is overhauling its economic policy

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.420
to turn back some of this free trade.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:21.030
And what's crazy is that both Republicans

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:24.930
and Democrats seem to agree
that we should do this and fast.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:26.130
(upbeat music)
- There is no reason.

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:27.603
We have the capacity.
(Congress applauds)

00:13:27.603 --> 00:13:30.300
- Some of these policies
include huge government

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.160
spending bills, like the CHIPS Act,

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:35.160
which would allocate over 50
billion government dollars

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.860
to companies that produce microchips

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.890
here in the United States,
instead of in Asia,

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:43.140
where the free market had
pulled them to set up.

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:44.970
This is also happening in other industries

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.040
that the government has deemed vital,

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:49.830
like critical minerals,
cars, and clean energy.

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:51.420
But they're not just giving subsidies,

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:53.220
like, it's not just
government money washing down

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:56.010
on all these industries,
it's also barriers.

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:58.710
Biden is imposing tariffs on imports,

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:01.950
which protects domestic
producers of certain industries

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:03.240
because they no longer have to compete

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:04.080
with the global market.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:05.820
They're also banning American companies

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:08.160
from selling certain products to China.

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:10.950
They're increasing
scrutiny on any investment

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:13.170
that comes in from places like China.

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:15.900
Like, this is a big shift.

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:18.180
And the question is, what happened?

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:20.910
How did we get from this free-trade heyday

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:23.903
that was supposed to usher
in world peace to this?

00:14:23.903 --> 00:14:24.780
(upbeat music)

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:27.060
- Markets have been whipsawed all morning,

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.550
and now afternoon, by this
ongoing trade war with China.

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:31.860
- The short answer is one word,

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:35.070
the answer to all of this: protection.

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:37.080
Here's my list of four protections

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:40.947
that best explain this big
shift in economic policy.

00:14:40.947 --> 00:14:42.540
(mysterious music)
First, workers,

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:44.070
which we already kind
of talked about briefly

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:45.150
with the China shock.

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:46.770
For all the winners of the global economy,

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:48.090
there were lots of losers,

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:49.590
and a lot of them were communities

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:51.270
who found themselves out of work

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:54.690
as their jobs went to
cheaper overseas locations.

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:57.420
Now listen, economists argue as to how big

00:14:57.420 --> 00:14:58.830
of a problem this actually was,

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:01.050
and you can read all
about it in my sources,

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:03.150
but the point is that closed factories

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:05.340
and unemployed communities
in the Rust Belt

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:07.860
have a major psychological
effect on people

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:10.290
who fear that their jobs
might just disappear,

00:15:10.290 --> 00:15:13.110
either abroad or be taken over by AI.

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:15.360
So the government is
trying to calm people down

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:17.460
by pumping money into certain sectors

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:19.320
to keep jobs in America.

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:21.540
The next big realization
about the global economy:

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:23.070
It doesn't really care much

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:26.190
about how much carbon it
pumps into our atmosphere.

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:29.940
Our world is barreling towards
a very real climate crisis,

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:31.620
and governments realize they're gonna have

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:33.690
to force the market to get serious

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:36.210
about things like electric
cars, solar panels,

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:37.680
and other green infrastructure.

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:39.900
The free market is not
going to incentivize this.

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:40.733
And if it doesn't happen,

00:15:40.733 --> 00:15:42.940
we're gonna have a much
bigger problem on our hands.

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:44.580
(mysterious music)

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:47.010
One of the best parts
of the global economy

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:49.920
is that our products get made by materials

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:52.890
that are being tossed around
the globe at the perfect time,

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:55.290
at the perfect place, to all come together

00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:56.910
to be made into a final product,

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:59.460
all to arrive at a shelf at some store

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:00.660
so that we can purchase it.

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:02.280
These supply chains are insane,

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:04.710
and they cross the entire planet.

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:06.270
And it works great

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:08.820
until the world shut down
in a global pandemic,

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:10.020
leaving people around the world

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:12.210
without the products
that they either needed

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:13.830
or just kinda got used to having.

00:16:13.830 --> 00:16:16.650
Again, economists argue about
how big of a deal this was.

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:17.483
A lot of them are like,

00:16:17.483 --> 00:16:19.590
"No, the supply chains held
up really well during COVID."

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:22.170
But regardless of what some
academic economist is saying

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:25.170
at MIT, all of us are left
with a pretty bad taste

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:27.480
in our mouth about supply
chains after COVID.

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:29.310
The system feels too fragile.

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:32.130
A lot of us feel too
reliant on a fragile system

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:33.810
of far away global connections

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:35.580
that could be rocked at any moment,

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:38.040
which has led to these
protectionist policies

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:41.190
that these governments hope
will bring supply chains back

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:42.600
inside of their borders.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:45.540
Okay, finally, and perhaps
the most important explanation

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:49.050
in all of this, is that Bill
Clinton was totally wrong.

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:50.970
Free trade didn't incentivize China

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:52.470
into a democratic revolution.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:53.910
The world did not join together

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:55.620
in one big free-trade market,

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:57.660
too entangled to fight with each other.

00:16:57.660 --> 00:16:59.790
In fact, quite the opposite has happened.

00:16:59.790 --> 00:17:02.490
- Relations between the
world's two largest economies

00:17:02.490 --> 00:17:04.500
are at their most tense in years.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:05.820
- After joining the global economy,

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:07.920
China got really rich really fast.

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:11.160
They've kind of started to
challenge the US at its own game,

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:13.320
and now they have one of
the biggest militaries

00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:15.120
on the planet, and they use that military

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:17.310
to assert their influence
around their region.

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:18.810
They use their newfound wealth

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:20.730
to project influence around the world

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:22.890
by building stuff or
giving loans to people.

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:25.500
And they're kind of trying
to dethrone the United States

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:27.390
as the one who's calling the shots

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:29.010
on the global economic order.

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:30.150
I made a whole video about this

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:31.350
that you can go watch if you want,

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:34.140
but the big point here
is that the US is worried

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:37.143
about the rapid rise of
their rival across the ocean,

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:40.350
especially when the rise of that rival

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:43.740
is in part fueled by American technology,

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:47.010
specifically these little
silicon chips, microchips,

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:48.720
that make weapons smart,

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:51.840
and that will be the brains
that power the AI revolution.

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:53.910
A lot of Joe Biden's
protectionist policies

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:56.040
are actually just aimed right at China,

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:58.740
trying to stop them
from using our own tech

00:17:58.740 --> 00:18:00.030
to get ahead of us.

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:01.740
Again, I made an entire
video about microchips.

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:03.366
You can go to that.

00:18:03.366 --> 00:18:05.100
I've kind of made a video
about a lot of these things.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:08.070
But yeah, Joe Biden is also
trying to get the supply chains

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:11.130
for these microchips out
of China's neighborhood,

00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:14.550
meaning out of Taiwan, bringing
them back inside our borders

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:17.100
to places like New Albany, Ohio,

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:19.380
nice and safe in the
middle of our continent.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:21.720
So you're gonna see a lot
more moments like this

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:24.930
with American presidents or
politicians with hard hats on,

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:27.060
speaking at construction sites.

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:30.540
- Folks, we need to make these
chips right here in America

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:33.780
to bring down everyday
cost and create good jobs.

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:36.907
America's back, and
America's leading the way.

00:18:36.907 --> 00:18:38.400
(tense music)

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:40.950
- This last point is kind
of a brutal recognition

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:43.410
for a lot of people,
including me, to be honest,

00:18:43.410 --> 00:18:46.530
that our theory that we
would all be more peaceful

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:50.700
if we were more economically
entangled is kind of wrong,

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:51.690
at least for now.

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:54.180
In fact, these economic
entanglements are looking

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:57.270
like they might turn
into tools of conflict

00:18:57.270 --> 00:18:59.490
as opposed to preventers of conflict.

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:01.740
Like, China has a near monopoly on a lot

00:19:01.740 --> 00:19:04.500
of the rare-earth elements
that we need to make a ton

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:07.290
of very important products,
including military weapons.

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:08.550
And if push comes to shove,

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:11.760
they could very realistically
use this as leverage

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:13.590
and cut us off in the exact same way

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:15.330
that we cut them off microchips.

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:16.650
I mean, this just happened.

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:18.720
Vladimir Putin turned off the pipelines

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:20.310
that pumped natural gas into Europe,

00:19:20.310 --> 00:19:22.290
the gas that is needed to heat the homes

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:23.460
and run the factories,

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:25.770
showing us that economic entanglements

00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:27.150
can actually be a weapon,

00:19:27.150 --> 00:19:29.010
and flipping this whole
theory on its head.

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:31.560
But yeah, Joe Biden is pouring money

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:33.300
into bringing production home,

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:35.790
and he has a lot of support
from all sides of the aisle.

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:37.590
So it's like economic nationalism.

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:40.710
The government is getting
involved to undo some

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:42.990
of what the free market did in its heyday,

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:45.870
which was to distribute
production of stuff

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:47.850
where it made the most economic sense,

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:49.680
not where it protected people.

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:52.110
- That means we will invent it in America

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:53.790
and make it in America,

00:19:53.790 --> 00:19:57.000
and we're gonna make sure
we include all of America.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:58.620
- So that's where we're at.

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:01.140
The world is reacting to
some weaknesses that they see

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:02.850
in the global free-trade system.

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:05.430
They're putting up barriers
in the name of protection.

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:07.980
And no one's really sure what that means.

00:20:07.980 --> 00:20:09.780
Like, it's kind of hard
to not look at this

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:11.880
and draw some parallels with the 1930s

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:15.060
when a series of economic
crises led countries

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:17.520
to put up trade barriers
in the name of protection,

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:20.670
which then led to ever
greater economic strife,

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:23.610
leading to unrest and
eventually culminating

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:25.590
in the Second World War.
(deflated music)

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:27.420
But don't read too far into that.

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:28.890
I'm not trying to like
do one of those things

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:30.210
where I'm like, "Every hundred years,

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:31.230
history repeats itself."

00:20:31.230 --> 00:20:33.390
That's not what I think is happening here.

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:34.920
But there's some similarities

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:36.870
that we should probably
just keep an eye on.

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:40.020
A suite of crises that
make us turn inward,

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:43.020
that surge populism and
economic nationalism

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:46.010
and the rise of tensions
between great powers.

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:46.950
(bright music)

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:48.600
Hey, before you go, I need
to tell you one last thing,

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:51.510
which is that we made
probably our last batch

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:53.850
of the poster that I designed last year.

00:20:53.850 --> 00:20:55.890
It's a poster called "All Maps Are Wrong,"

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:57.270
and I'm very proud of it.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:59.880
It shows all the ways that
you can take our Earth

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:02.250
and project it onto a 2D plane,

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:04.560
which always comes with some distortion.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:06.630
Anyway, it's kind of a
nerdy map-lover thing,

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:08.460
and if you're into that, you
should go over and check it out

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:09.360
before they're all gone.

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:10.710
This is probably gonna be our last batch,

00:21:10.710 --> 00:21:11.880
so get 'em while they last,

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:13.713
and the link's in the description.

